# Meals and events

This page aligns all the events of one kind at the time that they occurred. Then
it shows what occurred after them: the change in glucose, glucose, the
correction of the loop, the insulin above your schedule since the event, and
insulin and carbs on board. You can compare a maximum of three kinds of event
(cohorts).

The [Loop Analysis overview](/docs/reports/loop-analysis) tells you about the
filters, net loop correction and how to read the charts. They are the same on
all five pages.

<Screenshot id="loop-analysis-event-glucose-change" />

For each cohort, you can select a time of day. For meals, you can also select a
range of grams. Select the time to show before and after each event. The
default is 1 hour before and 5 hours after.

<Screenshot id="loop-analysis-meals-events" />

The report uses only events whose full window is before now. The report does
not use an event until its window is complete.

## The events that you can select

- **Meals**: a carb entry. Entries at the same time count as one meal.
- **Correction boluses**: a manual bolus that is not paired with a meal. No carb
  entry is linked to it, and no carbs are logged from 30 minutes before it to 1
  hour after it. Automatic boluses are not correction boluses.
- **Unannounced rises**: a rise in glucose that looks like a meal, with no carbs
  logged. See below.
- **Sensor starts**, and the start of **exercise**, an **override** or a
  **temporary target**.

## How the report finds an unannounced rise

The report looks for the pattern that a meal makes:

1. **A stable floor.** Glucose stays stable for 30 minutes or more. It moves
   20 mg/dL (1.1 mmol/L) or less. The lowest quarter of its readings is at
   70 mg/dL (3.9 mmol/L) or more.
2. **A climb.** After the floor, glucose increases by 40 mg/dL (2.2 mmol/L) or
   more in 45 minutes. If glucose goes back to the floor, the climb starts
   again.
3. **Not a sensor error.** The report does not use a climb faster than
   5 mg/dL (0.3 mmol/L) each minute. It does not use a single jump of
   30 mg/dL (1.7 mmol/L) or more. It also does not use the shape of a
   **compression recovery**. In this shape, glucose decreases quickly. Then it
   increases to the value from before the decrease. This is the shape that you
   get when you lie on your sensor.
4. **No carbs logged.** If carbs were logged from 3 hours before the rise to
   30 minutes after it, or carbs were on board, the report does not count the rise.

A second climb within 2 hours is part of the same rise.

You can also select **Only rises with extra loop insulin**.
Then the report uses only rises where the loop added half an hour of your usual
basal or more, and 0.2 U or more, in the 45 minutes after the rise started. This
removes rises that your loop did not treat as real.

## Rises and falls

<Screenshot id="loop-analysis-event-metrics" />

Each kind of event has a usual direction. After a meal, an unannounced rise or a
sensor start, the table shows rise figures. After a correction bolus, the start
of exercise, an override or a temporary target, the table shows fall figures.

For a rise, the table shows the **peak rise**, the **time to peak**, and the
time until glucose is again within 20 mg/dL (1.1 mmol/L) of the start glucose.

For a fall, the table shows these figures:

- **Drop to window end**: the start glucose minus the glucose at the end of the
  window. Settings evidence uses the same method over a maximum of 4 hours. The
  drop does not use the lowest reading, because noise can make the lowest
  reading too low.
- **Lowest glucose** and **Time to lowest glucose**.
- **Drop per unit above schedule**: the drop divided by the net insulin above
  your schedule. The insulin is for the full window of the event. This includes
  the time before the event. The table shows this figure only for isolated events with
  0.5 U or more above the schedule, and with no glucose below 70 mg/dL
  (3.9 mmol/L).
- **Went below range**: the number of events with a reading below 70 mg/dL
  (3.9 mmol/L) after the event.

## Isolated events

An event is **isolated** when no other meal, correction or meal-like rise occurs
in its window. To use only these events, select **Isolated events only**. If you
do not select it, the curves after a meal can also include the next snack.

The **observed grams per unit** is the grams of carbs for each unit of insulin
above your schedule in the window. It is not the same as your carb ratio, which
counts all of your bolus insulin. The figure shows only for isolated meals. When
a meal overlaps another event, the insulin in the window was not for that meal
only. So the grams divided by that insulin gives an incorrect value. The
figure also needs the delivery and the schedule for 90% of the window or more.

